// PARKED, NOT REGISTERED - and the blocker is named so the next reader does not
// rediscover it: at a kind collision the reference does not emit the user's
// declaration at all (the prelude struct takes the C name), while the port emits
// both under one name. Registering this file before that layer is mirrored would
// fail the suite for a reason it does not pin.
//
// What it WILL pin is the quiet face of kind collision, and it is the face worth
// a fixture. A user `enum Allocator` does not remove the prelude STRUCT of that
// name - the reference drops prelude declarations only within their own kind -
// so `*const Allocator` is the struct. Nothing here says `match`, nothing reads
// a field, nothing fails: with the binding wrong, BOTH compilers accepted this
// program and the parameter simply meant two different types. Only the emitted C
// said so, which is why parity is the assertion and exit status would have been
// silent throughout.

enum Allocator { A | B }

fn takes(x: *const Allocator) { println(1); }

fn main() { println(1); }
